  "dom_id": "step-q83584",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $6$, find the equation.",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $6.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $6.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
